    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> gcd(</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span>, </span><span><span class="hljs-number">6</span></span><span>q) = </span><span><span class="hljs-number">1</span></span><span> ⇒ Dirichlet ⇒ infinitos n ≡ </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> (mod </span><span><span class="hljs-number">6</span></span><span>q)

    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> n ≡ </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> (mod </span><span><span class="hljs-number">6</span></span><span>q) ⇒ n ∈ L4
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> n ∈ L4 ⇒ </span><span><span class="hljs-number">2</span></span><span>n+</span><span><span class="hljs-number">1</span></span><span> es compuesto por q

    Además:
       </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> n ≡ </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> (mod </span><span><span class="hljs-number">6</span></span><span>q) ⇒ n ∉ L3    // prueba modular → evita factorización L3

Por tanto:
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> n ∈ L4 ∧ n ∉ L3 ⇒ n ∈ L4\L2
    </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> existen infinitos n con estas propiedades ⇒ |L4\L2| = ∞
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
