</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> (m &lt; q ≤ n) ⇒ q | (n!/m!) ⇒ q | V.
</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> (m &lt; p ≤ n) ⇒ p | (n!/m!) ⇒ p | V.

</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> p y q son primos ⇒ p - q = </span><span><span class="hljs-number">2</span></span><span> ⇒ p,q son primos gemelos.

</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> ∈ {p,q} divide V Y </span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> no encaja en los factores permitidos según la forma L3/L4 ⇒ la factorización L3/L4 es imposible (</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">r</span></span><span> es testigo).

</span><span><span class="hljs-built_in">SI</span></span><span> además n&gt;</span><span><span class="hljs-number">2</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-literal">-2</span></span><span>&gt;m&gt;v</span><span><span class="hljs-literal">-1</span></span><span> entonces m∈{v,...,</span><span><span class="hljs-number">2</span></span><span>v</span><span><span class="hljs-literal">-3</span></span><span>} y se puede explorar p y q en relación a ese rango.
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
