04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copiar código</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre!"><span><span><span class="hljs-function">FUNC <span class="hljs-title">factor_log_sqrt</span></span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">E, B0=<span class="hljs-number">1000</span></span></span><span>, B1=</span><span><span class="hljs-number">1000000</span></span><span>):
    </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// 1) trivial factors small</span></span><span>
    factors = []
    </span><span><span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">for</span></span></span><span> p </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">primes_upto</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">B0</span></span><span>):
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">while</span></span><span> E % p == </span><span><span class="hljs-number">0</span></span><span>:
            factors.append(p)
            E </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">//= p</span></span><span>

    </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// 2) check perfect power</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">is_perfect_power</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">E</span></span><span>):
        (</span><span><span class="hljs-params">a,k</span></span><span>) = extract_perfect_power(E)
        factors += factor_log_sqrt(a, B0, B1) repeated k times
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">return</span></span><span> factors

    </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// 3) trial division up to B1</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">for</span></span></span><span> p </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">in</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">primes_between</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">B0+<span class="hljs-number">1</span></span></span><span>, B1):
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">while</span></span><span> E % p == </span><span><span class="hljs-number">0</span></span><span>:
            factors.append(p)
            E </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">//= p</span></span><span>

    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span><span> E == </span><span><span class="hljs-number">1</span></span><span>:
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">return</span></span><span> sorted(factors)

    </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// 4) if leftover is prime -&gt; done</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">is_probable_prime</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">E</span></span><span>):
        factors.</span><span><span class="hljs-title">append</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">E</span></span><span>)
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">return</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">sorted</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">factors</span></span><span>)

    </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// 5) use Pollard-Rho iterative until factored or time limit</span></span><span>
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">while</span></span><span> E &gt; 1 </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">and</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">not</span></span><span> </span><span><span class="hljs-title">is_probable_prime</span></span><span>(</span><span><span class="hljs-params">E</span></span><span>):
        d = pollard_rho(E)
        </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span><span> d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">is</span></span><span> None:
            </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// fall back: ECM or declare need GNFS</span></span><span>
            d = ecm_or_report(E)
            </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span><span> d </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">is</span></span><span> None: report_need_hard_factor(E); </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">break</span></span><span>
        </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">// recursively factor the discovered divisor</span></span><span>
        factors += factor_log_sqrt(d, B0, B1)
        E </span><span><span class="hljs-comment">//= d</span></span><span>

    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">if</span></span><span> E &gt; </span><span><span class="hljs-number">1</span></span><span>:
        factors.append(E)
    </span><span><span class="hljs-keyword">return</span></span><span> sorted(factors)
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start="5813" data-end="5840"><strong data-start="5813" data-end="5838">Funciones auxiliares:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="5841" data-end="6036">
<li data-start="5841" data-end="6036">
<p data-start="5843" data-end="6036"><code data-start="5843" data-end="5859">primes_upto(N)</code> (criba), <code data-start="5869" data-end="5887">is_perfect_power</code>, <code data-start="5889" data-end="5908">is_probable_prime</code> (Miller-Rabin), <code data-start="5925" data-end="5938">pollard_rho</code> (fácil de implementar, rápido en práctica), <code data-start="5983" data-end="5998">ecm_or_report</code> (usa biblioteca o reporta necesidad).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="6038" data-end="6083">3) Cómo usar logs para priorizar búsquedas</h2>
<ul data-start="6085" data-end="6659">
<li data-start="6085" data-end="6217">
<p data-start="6087" data-end="6217">Si tienes heurística que sugiere que un factor <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span></span></span></span> tiene tamaño aproximado <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{α}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">E</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.6644em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.0037em;">α</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>, entonces el log de <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span></span></span></span> es <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">α\log E</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.0037em;">α</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">E</span></span></span></span>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6218" data-end="6426">
<p data-start="6220" data-end="6426">Puedes dirigir Pollard-Rho/ECM con curvas / parámetros mayores para búsqueda de un rango de tamaños; ECM es muy bueno para factores del orden de 10–20 dígitos (práctico si esperas factores de ese tamaño).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6427" data-end="6659">
<p data-start="6429" data-end="6659">Para factorización de <code data-start="6451" data-end="6466">V = v*(n!/m!)</code> puedes estudiar <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log V = \log v + ∑_{k=m+1}^{n}\log k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.22222em;">V</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8889em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03588em;">v</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1623em; vertical-align: -0.358em;"></span><span class="mop"><span class="mop op-symbol small-op" style="position: relative; top: 0em;">∑</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8043em;"><span style="top: -2.4003em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span><span class="mrel mtight">=</span><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">m</span><span class="mbin mtight">+</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.2029em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">n</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.358em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mop">lo<span style="margin-right: 0.01389em;">g</span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.1667em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.03148em;">k</span></span></span></span> para estimar dónde caen factores primos grandes que provienen de este producto y priorizar pruebas sobre primos en ciertos intervalos.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="6661" data-end="6714">4) Uso concreto para el Método-V / Haus / Mersenne</h2>
<ul data-start="6716" data-end="7467">
<li data-start="6716" data-end="7246">
<p data-start="6718" data-end="6762"><strong data-start="6718" data-end="6759">Para E en Haus (producto de dos 6k±1)</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="6765" data-end="7246">
<li data-start="6765" data-end="6866">
<p data-start="6767" data-end="6866">Primero trial division hasta un umbral razonable para detectar factores pequeños (2,3,5,7,11...).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6869" data-end="6984">
<p data-start="6871" data-end="6984">Si Remainder R &gt; 1 y <code data-start="6892" data-end="6914">is_probable_prime(R)</code> → R es primo ⇒ E no factorizable en dos factores &gt;1 (o es primo*1).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6987" data-end="7246">
<p data-start="6989" data-end="7246">Si R compuesto, factorízalo con Pollard-Rho. Una vez obtengas factores, comprueba si hay particiones <code data-start="7090" data-end="7097">(A,B)</code> con A,B ≡ ±1 mod 6; si <strong data-start="7121" data-end="7132">ninguna</strong> partición cumple y además existe p|V que divide E y que no cae en ningún A o B posible → contradicción (testigo).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="7248" data-end="7467">
<p data-start="7250" data-end="7270"><strong data-start="7250" data-end="7267">Para Mersenne</strong>:</p>
<ul data-start="7273" data-end="7467">
<li data-start="7273" data-end="7467">
<p data-start="7275" data-end="7467">recuerda filtro <code data-start="7291" data-end="7307">r ≡ 1 (mod 2p)</code> para divisores auténticos. Si <code data-start="7338" data-end="7351">gcd(V, M_p)</code> produce r con esa congruencia, es candidato fuerte; factorizar M_p completo puede requerir ECM/MPQS si r es grande.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 data-start="7469" data-end="7514">5) Complejidad y recomendaciones prácticas</h2>
<ul data-start="7516" data-end="8031">
<li data-start="7516" data-end="7587">
<p data-start="7518" data-end="7587">Trial division hasta 10⁶ es trivial en tiempo si se hace con criba.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7588" data-end="7737">
<p data-start="7590" data-end="7737">Pollard-Rho factoriza enteros de 64 bits muy rápido; para 128+ bits usar ECM (implementaciones como <code data-start="7690" data-end="7697">pyecm</code> o <code data-start="7700" data-end="7708">msieve</code>) o GNFS para 200+ dígitos.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7738" data-end="8031">
<p data-start="7740" data-end="8031">Si trabajas con <code data-start="7756" data-end="7773">V = v * (n!/m!)</code>, <code data-start="7775" data-end="7778">V</code> puede ser gigantesco — <strong data-start="7802" data-end="7817">no intentes</strong> factorizar V entero cuando n es grande; mejor trabajar con <code data-start="7877" data-end="7888">gcd(V, E)</code> computado sin formar V entero (usa producto incremental y gcd como hicimos en el experimento). Eso es crítico: <strong data-start="8000" data-end="8030">no construiremos V literal</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="8033" data-end="8036">
<h1 data-start="8038" data-end="8090">¿Puedo hacerlo tú? — Sí, cómo proceder (tú puedes)</h1>
<p data-start="8092" data-end="8158">Sí — <strong data-start="8097" data-end="8107">puedes</strong> ejecutar esto localmente. Te doy las dos opciones:</p>
